WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
The idea of universal health care is often presented as a catch-all solution to America's health care problems,

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.000
often by more progressive politicians and political commentators.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.000
So let's talk about why universal health care is not necessarily the solution that we think it is.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.000
First of all, though, this is not necessarily an argument against universal health care.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Universal health care can be a really great thing, and people struggling to access health care in the United States

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.000
because of their financial situation is a huge problem that needs to be considered so, so much more.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.000
In fact, statistics for overall health, life expectancy, and maternal and infant mortality

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.180
infant mortality for populations and nations that have universal health care is very promising

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.080
and consistently better than nations that do not have universal health care.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.160
So it makes sense why so many liberal-minded Americans think universal health care is such

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.810
a great solution to America's health care problems.

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:40.170
But in reality, universal health care seems perfect until you're on a months-long wait

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.090
list for a surgery that has a huge impact on your life.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.170
In other words, many universal health care systems are accompanied by exhaustion of health

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.410
care workers, lower quality of care, and extremely long lead times.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.150
Despite its very apparent flaws, the US's market-based health care system has helped

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:54.210
drive medical innovation worldwide and gives patients fast access to specialists and elective

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:55.210
procedures.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.610
Obviously this becomes a huge issue when healthcare is focused more on profits than patients and

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.330
that's where we see huge problems within the US healthcare system.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:01.770
Basically what I'm trying to say here in this video is that the solution to the US's healthcare

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:05.050
problems is not black and white the way it is so often made out to be.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.530
Every system involves trade-outs but the best solution in terms of healthcare is likely

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.930
going to look like a balance between capitalist and socialist policies.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.490
The combination of universal coverage with private insurance and market elements pushes

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.570
that innovation that the US is so strong within the US but also gives people access despite

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:17.570
their financial situation.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.410
Obviously this is easier said than done but overall it's really important just to listen

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:23.330
to both sides of the argument because they both have their advantages and disadvantages.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:26.450
And like a lot of things in politics, the most perfect solution is going to be a balance

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:27.450
between both sides.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.450
Governing from the middle isn't just a political tool used to make everyone like you, it's

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.770
a political tool that's actually effective.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:32.770
So let's look at that with healthcare.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:33.770
Alright, that's all she wrote.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:33.790
Bye guys!
